reference_profiles._notes:
Optional Task 24 profile library, alongside (never instead of) corpus_profile above, which remains the primary profile and behaves exactly as before this key existed. Each alias here names one additional corpus profile grade.py will also compare a document against, producing an independent reference-fit reading per profile rather than one forced genre label -- see docs/experimental-tasks/24-*.md and textgrader/metrics/reference_fit.py. Never a normal metrics switch: it is a library of PROFILES, so it lives at the top level next to corpus_profile, not under 'metrics'. Example entry (a profile built with `python -m textgrader.corpus`, see corpus_builder/README.md -- never commit the built .json itself):
      "brown_news": {"path": "path/to/brown_news_profile.json", "label": "Brown Corpus: news", "genre": "news", "domain": "journalism", "period": "1961", "comparison_unit": "passage", "notes": "NLTK Brown Corpus, news category, built via corpus_builder/dataset_adapters.py"}
    Relative 'path' values resolve the same way corpus_profile's does: relative to the directory this config.json was loaded from.
